    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the New York state urban development corporation act, in relation to the capital access program and to provide for a new Innovate NY fund; and making appropriations for the support of government",
    "basePrintNo": "S5782",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5782-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §16-k, add §16-u, UDC Act",
    "lawSection": "New York State Urban Development Corporation Act",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5782\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the New York state urban development corporation act, in\nrelation to the capital access program and to provide for a new\nInnovate NY\nfund; and making appropriations for the\nsupport of government\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend New York State's Capital Access Program and\nestablish the Innovate NY Fund in order to make resources available to\nsupport emerging business ideas and products that result in the\ngrowth of business within New York State.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend New York State Urban Development\nCorporation Act (\"UDC Act\") § 16-k to enhance the ability of small\nbusinesses in New York State to access loan capital through the\nState's existing Capital Access Program. The legislation would expand\nthe group of New York-based financial institutions that can\nparticipate in the program by defining \"financial institution\" to\ninclude a \"community development financial institution or\ncommunity-based lending organization\" that is based principally in New\nYork State.\n\nSection 1 of the bill would also clarify that the purpose of the\nCapital Access Program is to assist small businesses that otherwise\nfind it difficult to obtain regular or sufficient bank financing.\nThis section would also require the New York State Urban Development\nCorporation doing business as Empire State Development (the\n\"Corporation\") to assure adequate geographic distribution of\nparticipating financial institutions throughout the State to the\nextent feasible.\n\nFinally, section 1 of the bill would authorize the Corporation to\ncontract with a third party financial institution to act as the\nCorporation's agent in administering the Capital Access Program and\nprovide that the selection of any such third party agent other than\nthe New York Business Development Corporation would be made pursuant\nto a competitive process.\n\nSection 2 of this bill would amend the UDC Act by adding a new section\n16-u that establishes the Innovate NY Fund.\n\nSubdivision 1 of section 16-u states that the purpose of the Innovate\nNY fund is to make available state funds to eligible applicants to\nsupport emerging business ideas and products that result in the\ngrowth of business within the state and the concomitant creation of\njobs and tax revenues for the State.\n\n\nSubdivision 2 of section 16-u would provide that eligible applicants\nfor Innovate NY funds may include economic development organizations,\ntechnology development organizations, research universities and\ninvestment funds that provide seed-stage investments in New York\ncompanies.\n\nSubdivision 3 of section 16-u would establish the requirements for the\nultimate beneficiaries of an investment. These would include that the\nentity must currently be, or will agree in writing to be, located\nwithin the State; the entity must be in the seed-stage of development\nas defined by the Corporation; and the entity must demonstrate a\npotential for substantial growth and job development and have the\npotential to generate additional economic activity in New York State.\nThis subdivision would provide that priority be given to companies\ninvolved in commercialization of research and development or\nhigh-tech manufacturing.\n\nSubdivision 4 of section 16-u would provide for the Corporation to\nestablish a competitive process for receiving and evaluating\napplications for the Innovate NY Fund. This subdivision would also\nprovide that the applications be evaluated based on certain criteria\nand sets forth certain of those criteria. The subdivision states that\nthe Corporaticm is to assure that applicants demonstrate the need for\nseed capital in the areas served and provide for adequate geographic\ndistribution of awards throughout the State to the extent feasible.\nIt would also require the Corporation to distribute funds promptly\npursuant to a disbursement process agreed to between the Corporation\nand the applicant to enable the applicant to fulfill commitments to\nbeneficiary companies in a timely manner.\n\nSubdivision 5 of section 16-u would require that investments of\nInnovate NY funds in beneficiary companies be leveraged with private\nsources of capital at a ratio equal to or greater than two to one\n(2:1). The leveraged match would be met on an aggregate basis and\nexcludes investments after the initial funding round. Compliance with\nthe match requirement would be determined by the Corporation when the\napplicant has invested fifty percent of the Innovate NY funds\ncommitted to such applicant and annually thereafter.\n\nSubdivision 6 of section 16-u would impose limitations on the amount\nof Innovate NY funds that an applicant may invest in any single\nbeneficiary company.\n\nSubdivision 7 of section 16-u would authorize the Corporation to\nestablish an Innovate NY program fund and to pay into such fund any\neligible resources available to the Corporation from any source,\nincluding appropriations made by the State.\n\nSubdivision 8 of section 16-u would require the Corporation to submit\na report annually on December 31 to the Director of the Budget, the\nTemporary President of the Senate, the Speaker of the Assembly, the\nMinority Leader of the Senate and the Minority Leader of the Assembly\nproviding information on investments made by, and the results\nachieved through, the Innovate NY Fund.\n\nSubdivision 9 of section 16-u would authorize the Corporation to\npromulgate rules and regulations to implement this legislation,\n\n\nincluding rules and regulations with respect to the\nreasonable management and other fees and charges of applicants that\nreceive Innovate NY funds as well as the repayment of funds received\nby a beneficiary company if the beneficiary company leaves the State\nwithin a certain period of time to be determined by the Corporation.\n\nSubdivision 10 of section 16-u would permit the Corporation to impose\nfees, establish repayment terms and provide for equity participation\nby the Corporation in connection with investments from the Innovate\nNY Fund in accordance with rules and regulations to be promulgated by\nthe Corporation.\n\nSubdivision 11 of section 16-u would exempt the Corporation from the\nprovisions of sections 10 and 16(2) of the UDC Act.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would provide for an appropriation of the\nfederal monies received by the Department of Economic Development to\nbe paid to the Corporation to fund the Capital Access Program, the\nInnovate NY Fund and the Corporation's Bonding Guarantee\nAssistance Program.\n\nSection 4 would make the bill effective immediately.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nSection 16-k of the UDC Act provides for the current Capital Access\nProgram.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nOn September 27, 2010, President Obama signed into law the Small\nBusiness Jobs Act of 2010 (the \"Act\") to help increase credit\navailability for small businesses. The Act included the State Small\nBusiness Credit Initiative (\"SSBCI\"), a $1.5 billion program\nadministered by the U.S. Department of the Treasury (\"Treasury\")\nintended to provide direct support to states for use in programs\ndesigned to increase access to credit for small businesses. Pursuant\nto the Act, Treasury allocated funds to the states according to a\nstatutory formula. New York is eligible to receive approximately\n$55.4 million and no state matching funds are required.\n\nThe SSBCI permits funding for two state program categories: capital\naccess programs (\"CAPs\") and other credit support programs (\"OCSPs\").\nThis bilI would allow New York State to employ a portion of its\nfederal SSBCI allocation in the State's existing Capital Access\nProgram. In addition, this bill would amend current law to make the\nexisting program more accessible to small businesses in New York State.\n\nThe existing Capital Access Program is inactive. This bill would\nreinvigorate the Capital Access Program by strengthening the statute\ngoverning its function and by providing for new funding. This would\ngreatly assist small businesses by enhancing the climate for lending\nin traditional and alternative financial institutions.\n\nIn addition to funding the Capital Access Program, it is expected that\nfederal SSBCI resources will be used to fund the Corporation's\nbonding guarantee assistance program. This\n\n\nprogram will aid small businesses, including minority-owned and\nwomen-owned firms, in obtaining the necessary bonding authority to\nsecure State contracts.\n\nThis bill would also establish the Innovate NY Fund. The Fund would\nleverage federal SSBCI funding by providing funding to entities,\nincluding regional and local economic development organizations,\ntechnology development organizations, research universities and\ninvestment funds, which would use the funding to make seed-stage\ninvestments in New York State companies.\n\nThe Innovate NY Fund would invest approximately $25 million with firms\ninvesting in seed-stage businesses in New York that have developed\ncutting-edge breakthroughs in emerging technologies. To maximize the\neffectiveness of available resources, an applicant seeking Innovate\nNY funds would be evaluated based on a number of criteria. The\nInnovate NY Fund, supported through an allocation of SSBCI federal\nresources and appropriately leveraged with significant private\ninvestment, would increase the State's ability to make much-needed\ninvestments that will enhance the tremendous intellectual capital and\ninnovative spirit of New York State.\n\nTogether, these provisions would help ensure that New York State\nremains \"Open for Business,\" by creating a climate within which\ninnovation, creativity and growth can flourish.\nThe bill would provide a system within which public-private\npartnerships could be fostered in a manner that capitalizes on the\nbusiness and university infrastructure that anchors economic\ndevelopment in New York. This strategy will encourage economic growth\nin New York State.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThere are no fiscal implications from this bill.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis bill takes effect immediately.\n\n",
